From: Construct <release@constructlinux.org>
Subject: API: Keep watching the panel when the clock is already in place

clockMenuPositionSet() keeps the date menu at its offset by watching the
panel boxes' child-added, and dropped that watch first thing on every
call: when the clock was already where it should be, it returned before
connecting it again. An item an extension added after that - Vitals,
which inserts itself at the right box's start - then sat before the
clock for good, and whether it did depended on the order the shell's
extensions happened to come up in: one boot had the clock before Vitals'
readings, the next after them.

Drop the watch only when the clock is moved, which connects it again.

--- a/src/lib/API.js
+++ b/src/lib/API.js
@@ -1480,8 +1480,6 @@ export class API
             this._main.panel._leftBox,
         ];
 
-        this.#disconnectClockMenuPositionSignals();
-
         let fromPos = -1;
         let fromIndex = -1;
         let toIndex = -1;
@@ -1507,6 +1505,9 @@ export class API
             return;
         }
 
+        // Watched again once moved: a clock already in its place keeps the
+        // watch, so an item a later extension inserts before it is undone.
+        this.#disconnectClockMenuPositionSignals();
         panelBoxes[fromPos].remove_child(dateMenu.container);
         panelBoxes[pos].insert_child_at_index(dateMenu.container, toIndex);
 
